                    coach_default:          `Rebalance logged.`,
                    coach_r1_esgtilt:       `<strong>That's the right attribution.</strong> This is not a stock-picking failure — it is your <em>ESG sector tilt</em> meeting an asymmetric shock. Diagnose it correctly and you can decide deliberately how much of the tilt to give back, rather than panic-selling the wrong thing.`,
                    coach_r1_selection:     `<strong>Blaming stock selection mis-reads the drawdown.</strong> The names did roughly what their sectors did — renewables, staples and healthcare were hit while energy and materials rebounded. Treat this as a picking error and you will rebalance into the wrong fix.`,
                    coach_r1_factor:        `<strong>“It will mean-revert on its own” is a dangerous bet 17 days from an audit.</strong> The factor and sector exposure is structural to your ESG tilt — hoping it reverses does nothing for a 22% volatility breach that is live right now.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> ESG-tilt attribution, the live volatility breach correctly identified, and the two controversy names flagged as covenant landmines rather than cheap positions. That is exactly the read that lets you rebalance deliberately instead of reactively.`,
                    coach_r1_breachdd:      `<strong>Drawdown is dangerous, but it is not the live breach.</strong> At 12.4% you still have ~260 bps to the 15% stop. Volatility at 22% is <em>already</em> through the 18% ceiling — that is the constraint out of compliance today.`,
                    coach_r1_breachesg:     `<strong>The ESG score is at risk, not yet breached.</strong> You are 3 points above the 70th-percentile floor. The live breach is volatility at 22%. Misidentify which limit binds and you will spend headroom you do not have.`,
                    coach_r1_landmine:      `<strong>Treating the controversy names as landmines is the right instinct.</strong> A position you can sell; a landmine detonates on someone else's timetable. With agencies flagging a downgrade in 30 days, these two are forward covenant risk, not value plays.`,
                    coach_r1_ordinary:      `<strong>“They look cheap, hold them” is how a position becomes a landmine.</strong> The agencies have flagged both for downgrade within 30 days. If one moves, it can push your whole weighted score through the floor — and you will be forced to sell into a downgrade everyone can see.`,
                    coach_r1_add:           `<strong>Adding to the controversy names doubles down on the landmine.</strong> You are buying more of exactly the exposure that can detonate your covenant. Cheap is not the same as safe when a downgrade is 30 days away.`,
                    coach_r1_headroom:      `<strong>You have over-stated your drawdown headroom.</strong> 15.0% − 12.4% is only ~260 bps. Believe you have more and you will size trades you cannot afford if the market drops 2% more before the audit.`,
                    coach_r2_ccc:           `<strong>Opening the book to ≥CCC buys back return — and arms the redemption clause.</strong> You now have full access to the rebounding carbon-heavy names, but your weighted ESG score is sliding toward the 70th-percentile floor. Was the recovered return worth handing ฿12bn the right to walk at no penalty?`,
                    coach_r2_bb:            `<strong>Relaxing to ≥BB reaches for the rebounders — the classic return-chase.</strong> Volatility and the gap improve fast, but every low-ESG name you add drags the weighted score toward the floor. The benchmark is not your binding constraint; the covenant is.`,
                    coach_r2_bbb:           `<strong>Holding ≥BBB keeps the universe genuinely sustainable.</strong> You give up some speed of return recovery, but you protect the weighted score and keep the covenant out of immediate danger. This is the disciplined dial — now make the CSR weight match it.`,
                    coach_r2_a:             `<strong>Tightening to ≥A maximizes integrity but concentrates your risk.</strong> Your ESG score looks great — and your volatility just climbed because you shed diversification into a handful of high-rated names. Integrity that breaches the 18% ceiling does not help your clients if the book gets stopped out.`,
                    coach_r2_disciplined:   `<strong>That is the disciplined rebalance.</strong> A ≥BBB threshold with the controversy exposure capped near zero protects the covenant <em>and</em> defuses the landmine before the agencies act. You traded a little return recovery for a covenant you can actually defend at the audit.`,
                    coach_r2_trim:          `<strong>Trimming the CSR exposure is a reasonable half-step.</strong> You have reduced the landmine without fully defusing it. If an agency downgrades before the audit, a partial position can still move your weighted score — decide whether half-out is enough.`,
                    coach_r2_csrfull:       `<strong>Holding both controversy names at full weight leaves the landmine armed.</strong> You are 17 days from the audit with two positions an agency may downgrade any day. Keep them at full weight and you are betting the covenant on the agencies' timing.`,
                    coach_r3_oneday:        `<strong>A one-day exit cleans up the score fast — and pays for it.</strong> You took ~150 bps of market impact on the 4.2% position, roughly ฿11M straight out of NAV. Smart on compliance, but be sure fear of the audit did not just cost your clients real money a staged sale would have saved.`,
                    coach_r3_staged:        `<strong>Staging the exit saves most of the market impact.</strong> You restore the score toward the floor while paying a fraction of the one-day cost. The risk is a worse downgrade mid-sale — you have bet that liquidity holds long enough to work the position out.`,
                    coach_r3_hold:          `<strong>Holding the downgraded name is the landmine detonating in your hands.</strong> Your live ESG score is already below the floor and you are riding it into the audit. Hoping the downgrade reverses is not a risk policy — it is the third common error made in real time.`,
                    coach_r3_dumplowesg:    `<strong>Buying more low-ESG rebounders with the score already below floor is doubling down on the breach.</strong> You narrowed the return gap and pushed the covenant further under water two weeks before the audit. The benchmark is not the constraint that can redeem ฿12bn.`,
                    coach_r3_nothing:       `<strong>Hoping the index reconstitution lifts you back above floor is not a plan.</strong> Your live score is below the covenant floor now. Reconstitution is weeks away and outside your control — restore the score with trades you actually make, before the 31 March strike.`,
                    coach_r3_hedge:         `<strong>The volatility overlay is the missing piece.</strong> A hedge sleeve pulls volatility back under the 18% ceiling without forcing you to buy low-ESG names for the de-risking. That is how you respect the risk budget and the covenant at the same time.`,
                    coach_r3_lever:         `<strong>Adding leverage to chase the gap is the risk budget in reverse.</strong> You narrowed the return gap, but volatility and drawdown both climbed toward their stops. Two weeks from the audit, leverage is exactly the wrong tool — one more 2% down day and the board hard stop liquidates your book.`,
                    coach_r3_nooverlay:     `<strong>No overlay leaves volatility to chance.</strong> You are hoping the 30-day number falls by audit day on its own. A hedge sleeve would have taken the volatility breach off the table deliberately rather than leaving it to the market.`,
                    coach_r3_clean:         `<strong>A clean execution.</strong> Staged exit to save impact, proceeds rotated into high-rated names to lift the score above floor, and a volatility overlay to pull the book back under the ceiling. You restored the covenant and respected the risk budget in the same move.`,
                    coach_r4_framework:     `<strong>A standing framework is what defends the mandate.</strong> A permanent threshold, a capped CSR-exposure budget, a buffer above the floor, a volatility overlay and covenant monitoring mean a single downgrade can never again arm the redemption clause. That is the policy a sophisticated LP will renew.`,
                    coach_r4_atfloor:       `<strong>Rebalancing to land exactly at the floor is fragile by design.</strong> Covenant satisfied, technically — with zero buffer. One downgrade, one index reconstitution, one bad print and you breach. Professional ESG mandates carry a cushion for exactly this reason.`,
                    coach_r4_loosest:       `<strong>Dropping the ESG threshold permanently abandons the mandate you are defending.</strong> You may never lag the benchmark again, but you are no longer the ESG Leaders Fund — and the European LPs bought integrity, not a closet index tracker. This pitch loses the very capital it is trying to keep.`,
                    coach_r4_riskadjusted:  `<strong>That is the mandate defense that holds.</strong> You showed the ESG tilt was managed through the drawdown, the covenant held with a buffer, and the risk budget was respected — integrity as <em>risk-adjusted</em> value, not a cost or a slogan. The LP can renew on this.`,
                    coach_r4_return:        `<strong>Leading with return alone misreads what these LPs bought.</strong> They can see the gap closed — but they embedded an ESG covenant, not a return target. Lead with performance and ignore the integrity story, and you invite the question of why they pay you instead of a tracker.`,
                    coach_r4_apology:       `<strong>Apologizing for the ESG tilt concedes the whole thesis.</strong> The tilt was the drawdown <em>and</em> the reason these LPs are invested. Call it a mistake and quietly dilute it, and you have told your covenant-bound clients you no longer believe in the product they bought.`,
                    coach_r4_premium:       `<strong>“ESG is a free return premium” is the claim this very disruption just refuted.</strong> Your high-ESG tilt is exactly what drew the drawdown. Sarajoti's point is sharper and more defensible: ESG protects value in the downside and is state-dependent — it is risk management, not free alpha.`,
                    coach_r4_cost:          `<strong>Framing ESG as a pure cost undersells your own discipline.</strong> If it were only a cost, the rational move is to drop it. The defensible thesis is that ESG and CSR discipline price controversy and political/regulatory risk — value protected, not value sacrificed.`,

